{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of the boundary-objects note revision at head 34bc5b5a84150958f6a709c4671ff2b556940b40, for request bc4aa093 and review request 596f4565. Verdict: CHANGES REQUESTED.\n\nThe head is one commit on main 41da9810 and changes one file, `notes/2026-10-28-boundary-objects-in-use.md`. I read the note at the head first and then its diff from 41da9810. I checked the \"What changes where\" table and the consent rule against main 41da9810.\n\nThe cut is right. A recipe as a note, consent by signing the package act, and a note fact as a locator base remove an item type, three acts, four guards and five refusal names. No case in sections 3 and 4 loses its path. Section 3.3 and W3 bind each party by its own signature on an act that names the terms by fact. That is what the ratification gave, because the ratification could only follow the item's opening. Two facts at main and one writing point need a recut.\n\n## Changes requested\n\n1. The catalog count. The table says the catalog pins 76 versions and 77 after. At 41da9810, `docs/kernel.md` line 112 says 80. The evidence-rules landing 9e313eff raised it from 76, and `native-catalog.ts` now imports its cohort, Rules scope 13 and Destination 17. The row should read 80 today and 81 after. The Source line still names main a78e0981, and it should name 41da9810 for the facts that the revision rechecked.\n2. `consent-unstated` reaches packages on main. The rule refuses \"a slot with no `consent` and an effect that writes a member other than the signer\". On main, these effects write a party from a field or from another slot:\n   - `apps/counter/definition.ts`: `controller` from the field `opener` (line 67), and `speaker` and `lastSpeaker` from other slots (lines 30, 39 and 101);\n   - `apps/room/lanes/src/change.ts`: `author` from `opener` (line 282), `requested` from a field (lines 868 and 880), and `informed` from other items' slots (line 1032).\n\n   Under BO3, the package check and the install refuse all of them. The \"What changes where\" table and the BO3 slice should name these definitions and say what each slot declares, which is most likely `declared`. A slot that only clears its member (`from: null`) writes no member and needs nothing.\n3. Writing, under the AGENTS.md rule.\n   - Some sentences describe the note's own history instead of its subject:\n     - the status paragraph after \"Nothing here is built\";\n     - the opening of section 9 from \"The first version of this section\";\n     - the last five sentences of \"How a package binds a party\", from \"The first version of this section had a third value\".\n   - The design now stands without them. The review record and the commit message hold that history.\n   - The status line says that \"this note changes no landed document\", but the note is itself a landed document. \"Changes no other document\" is correct.\n\n## Checks at this head\n\n- The worktree is clean. The head merges clean onto main 41da9810.\n- `packages/contract/src/definition.ts` has `parties: Record\u003cstring, SlotRule \u0026 { list; max?; author }\u003e` and no locator.\n- `PlatformDefinition` in `packages/contract/src/scope.ts` names eight parts.\n- `creation-versions.ts` has no commons. The three Workers each list six historical pins in `PLATFORM_CREATION_PINS`.\n- The chat-composed views note defines `keep-view` as a `view` item act. No code outside notes and docs names `keep-view`, `share-view` or `drop-view`.\n- `restricted@1` is the expression profile of `application-view.ts`.\n- W2 and W3 still prove what BO2 and BO3 need. W3 refuses B's act under `signer` and refuses the second package with `consent-unstated`.\n- check-doc-links on an export of the head: 736 links, 0 broken.\n- check-notes: 0 warnings.\n- check-commit-messages: 1 checked, 0 flagged.\n- `git diff --check`: clean.\n- No prose line is over 79 characters.\n- The note names no product. Ink \u0026 Switch, Potluck and Webstrates appear in Source as research citations, in the same way as Bowker and Star, and the body makes no comparison.\n\n## Points that do not block\n\n1. The `about` cap. `about` holds at most 16 entries, but section 3.3 reads 23 photos. A reading whose rows cite photos inside its text does not name them in `about`, so a projection of one photo does not list the reading. Say whether a reading over more than 16 documents splits, or whether the projection also reads row citations.\n2. The grant for signing. Under `signer`, a party binds only by an act whose grant it holds. The removed `ratified` path let a member with only `commons.write` bind itself. Say that the package grants each party slot's writing act to the role that fills it.\n3. What reads `ratify`. No consent rule spends a ratification now. Section 9 keeps `close` as an act because a projection reads one bit from it. Name the projection or rule that reads `ratify`, such as an approved exact text in W1, or fold it into `note`.\n4. The journal place. The entry range `{ scope, from, to }` carries its own scope, so it has no document digest or note fact as its base. Say which base it takes, or make the journal scope a third base.\n5. `consent: signer` and the existing parts. Party slots already have `author`, and effects already have `from: { signer: true }`. Say how `consent` relates to both, or whether `signer` means \"every effect that writes this slot has `from: { signer: true }`\".\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe revision makes the commons smaller and puts each thing where it already lives. A recipe is text, so it is a note. Binding is a signature, so it is the package act. Row identity comes from citations, so the host keeps it as a convention. The four steps from a view to a package follow the gentle slope that the note cites. Approved, subject to item 2, which changes what BO3 touches.\n\n## Security\n\n- Binding needs the party's own signature on an entry that names the terms by fact. The design no longer has a presented-signature path to get wrong.\n- `declared` slots still appear on the install screen.\n- A recipe in a shared room names private notes by fact reference, never by text digest.\n- Computed columns write nothing.\n- Item 2 matters here as well. Without it, BO3 either breaks the Counter and the lanes, or tempts a waiver of the rule.\n\nApproved, subject to item 2.\n\n## Simplification\n\nThe revision removes one item type, three acts, one consent value, four guards, a marker, an entry part and five refusal names. It adds one locator base and one convention. That simplifies the design. Approved.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: approved, subject to item 2.\n- Security: approved, subject to item 2.\n- Simplification: approved.\n- The request's checks:\n  - no case in sections 3 and 4 loses a path;\n  - W2 and W3 hold;\n  - the table does not match main 41da9810 (items 1 and 2);\n  - sections 8 and 9 need item 3.\n\nA recut that makes items 1 to 3 can come back for a short review of the diff.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:394d48bf12de9deb48ced195bc5703a68b5f7179","binding":"assigned","head":"34bc5b5a84150958f6a709c4671ff2b556940b40","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:feb978f65375483660d14cead9b32788a5f722a6\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:0072639d674d37e8b6686dcf7d43b9c625363c44\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:cf955132213fdb1f494a23123dcb793ca181efed","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}